{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:52:55Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 14, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\t# Implementation: Make the app usable from the keyboard\n2\t\n3\t**Taiga:** #14\n4\t**Spec:** `docs/spec.md`\n5\t**Design:** `docs/design.md`\n6\t**Branch:** feature/tp-14/make-app-usable-from-keyboard\n7\t\n8\t## Summary\n9\t\n10\tEvery control was already a native, keyboard-operable element, so this cycle\n11\tadds the three things that were actually missing: an initial focus target\n12\t(AC1), a guaranteed-visible `:focus-visible` style including the two dark\n13\tbutton backgrounds the default outline is weak against (AC2), and explicit\n14\tfocus restoration after the three actions that used to drop focus to\n15\t`<body>` \u2014 Delete, Done (when the row disappears), and closing the edit\n16\tform via Escape/Cancel/Save (AC4\u2013AC7). No markup, ARIA, or visual design\n17\tchanged beyond the focus indicator itself.\n18\t\n19\t## Changes by file\n20\t\n21\t### `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx`\n22\t- Added `autoFocus` to the task-title input (AC1).\n23\t- Wrapped the component in `forwardRef` + `useImperativeHandle`, exposing a\n24\t  `.focus()` method, so `App.jsx` can return focus to this input once the\n25\t  task list empties out (AC4's last fallback) without reaching into the\n26\t  component's internal DOM. This is the option the design doc named\n27\t  explicitly (\"Developer choice: either `useImperativeHandle` or a ref via\n28\t  `forwardRef`\").\n29\t\n30\t### `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx`\n31\t- `TaskList` and `TaskRow` now accept a `registerRowButtonRef(id, kind, el)`\n32\t  callback prop, threaded down from `App.jsx`.\n33\t- Each row's Done and Delete buttons pass themselves to\n34\t  `registerRowButtonRef(task.id, 'done'|'delete', el)` via a callback ref.\n35\t  React calls this with `null` on unmount, which the App-side registry uses\n36\t  to clean up its bookkeeping.\n37\t- `TaskRow` keeps a local `editButtonRef` (a plain `useRef`, not lifted to\n38\t  `App`, since the Edit button belongs to the same component instance across\n39\t  the `isEditing` toggle \u2014 it never unmounts as a *component*, only its\n40\t  rendered subtree changes shape). A `useEffect` watching `isEditing` calls\n41\t  `editButtonRef.current?.focus()` whenever `isEditing` transitions from\n42\t  `true` to `false` (Escape, Cancel, or a successful Save), covering AC6 and\n43\t  AC7 with one code path. A `skipNextFocusRestore` ref guards against this\n44\t  effect firing on the row's initial mount (where `isEditing` starts\n45\t  `false`), which would otherwise steal focus from the AC1 autofocus target\n46\t  on every page load.\n47\t\n48\t### `frontend/src/App.jsx`\n49\t- Added `addTaskRef` (passed to `AddTask`) and `rowButtonRefs` \u2014 a\n50\t  `useRef(new Map())` keyed by `task.id`, each entry `{ delete, done }`\n51\t  pointing at that row's live button elements. Kept as a ref, not state,\n52\t  since it's DOM bookkeeping that shouldn't itself cause re-renders.\n53\t- `registerRowButtonRef(id, kind, el)`: writes/clears one entry per\n54\t  register/unregister call.\n55\t- `computeFocusFallback(id, kind)`: given the row being removed and which\n56\t  control \"kind\" to prefer, looks at `sortedTasks` (the same array passed to\n57\t  `TaskList`, so it matches on-screen order under the current filter/search)\n58\t  and returns the next row's `{id, kind}`, else the previous row's, else\n59\t  `{ type: 'addTask' }`. This must be computed *before* the delete/complete\n60\t  API call, since afterwards the row is gone from `sortedTasks`.\n61\t- `handleDelete(id)`: computes the fallback with `kind: 'delete'` (AC4 always\n62\t  targets the neighbour's Delete button), performs the delete, and on\n63\t  success stores the fallback as a pending `focusRequest`.\n64\t- `handleComplete(id)`: only computes a fallback (`kind: 'done'`) when\n65\t  `filter === 'open'`, i.e. only when completing the task will actually\n66\t  remove the row from view. If the filter is `'all'` (the only other filter\n67\t  a Done click is reachable from \u2014 a task can't be completed from a `'done'`\n68\t  filter, since it wouldn't be visible there yet), no fallback is computed\n69\t  and no `focusRequest` is set, per AC5's \"no special handling needed\" case.\n70\t- A `useEffect` on `[tasks, focusRequest]` resolves any pending\n71\t  `focusRequest` once the post-action render has committed: for\n72\t  `type: 'addTask'` it calls `addTaskRef.current.focus()`; for `type: 'row'`\n73\t  it looks up `rowButtonRefs.current.get(id)`, focuses that row's `kind`\n74\t  button, falling back to that row's Delete button if the preferred kind\n75\t  isn't present (the \"Done-or-Delete-position control\" wording in AC5 \u2014 e.g.\n76\t  the next row happens to already be done and has no Done button) or to the\n77\t  AddTask input as a last-resort safety net if the row's entry is missing\n78\t  entirely. It then clears `focusRequest`.\n79\t- `<AddTask ref={addTaskRef} .../>` and\n80\t  `<TaskList ... registerRowButtonRef={registerRowButtonRef} onComplete={handleComplete} onDelete={handleDelete} .../>`\n81\t  wire the above into the render tree. `onUpdate`/`onPriorityChange`/\n82\t  `onDueDateChange` are unchanged.\n83\t\n84\t### `frontend/src/App.css`\n85\tAppended after the existing rules (`App.css:153` on):\n86\t\n87\t```css\n88\t*:focus-visible {\n89\t  outline: 2px solid #0066cc;\n90\t  outline-offset: 2px;\n91\t  box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px #fff;\n92\t}\n93\t\n94\t.filter-option.active:focus-visible,\n95\t.priority-select.priority-high:focus-visible {\n96\t  outline: 2px solid #fff;\n97\t  outline-offset: -4px;\n98\t  box-shadow: none;\n99\t}\n100\t```\n101\t\n102\tThe first rule is the design doc's global `:focus-visible` treatment,\n103\tunchanged. The second rule is a deviation \u2014 see below.\n104\t\n105\t## Deviation from `docs/design.md`\n106\t\n107\tThe design's global rule pairs a 2px blue outline (offset 2px out) with a\n108\t2px white `box-shadow`, reasoning that the white ring gives ~7:1 contrast\n109\tagainst `.filter-option.active` (`#16191c`) and ~2.7:1 against\n110\t`.priority-select.priority-high` (`#a8480a`).\n111\t\n112\tI was asked to sanity-check this before shipping it as-is, because an\n113\toutset `box-shadow` with no offset/blur paints in the 2px band *immediately\n114\toutside* the element's border box \u2014 not over the element's own fill. For\n115\tmost controls that band sits over `.task { background: #fff }` or the page\n116\tbackground (`#f6f7f8`), directly touching the button's edge, so the ring\n117\tstill reads as a real edge against a dark fill in isolation.\n118\t\n119\t`.filter-option` is the case where this breaks down further: filter buttons\n120\tare laid out flush against each other with a 1px separator border and no\n121\tgap (`App.css:65-74`, `.task-filter { display: inline-flex; ... overflow:\n122\thidden }`). When the focused button's box-shadow spreads 2px into a\n123\tneighbour's space, that space is painted over the *next* sibling's own\n124\topaque background as browsers paint later-DOM-order siblings on top \u2014 so\n125\tpart of the ring can be visually clipped or bled into the adjacent button\n126\tinstead of forming a clean ring, on top of the fact that the ring's outer\n127\tedge is barely distinguishable from the surrounding page background either\n128\tway.\n129\t\n130\tFor these two selectors only, I replaced the outset white box-shadow with\n131\tan **inset** white outline (`outline-offset: -4px`) instead. An inset\n132\toutline is drawn entirely inside the control's own border box, directly\n133\tagainst its own dark fill, so it can't bleed into or be occluded by a flush\n134\tneighbour, and its contrast doesn't depend on whatever happens to be\n135\tpainted behind the element. `.priority-select.priority-low` (light grey,\n136\t`#e8eaec`) and every other control keep the design's original global rule \u2014\n137\tthe blue outline alone is already \"soft but perceptible\" there per the\n138\tdesign's own contrast table, matching the plain-white-background case, and\n139\tthe spec only calls out the near-black and dark-orange backgrounds as\n140\tneeding extra treatment.\n141\t\n142\tI don't have browser/rendering tooling in this environment to screenshot\n143\tthe result, so this is a reasoned-through adjustment, not a visually\n144\tconfirmed one \u2014 worth a look once it's running, per the request that\n145\tflagged this.\n146\t\n147\t## Other notes / non-deviations\n148\t\n149\t- AC5's \"no special handling needed\" case (filter `'all'`, row stays\n150\t  visible, only the Done button disappears) is implemented literally as\n151\t  the spec decided it: `handleComplete` doesn't set a `focusRequest` in\n152\t  that branch, so the browser's own default behaviour applies when a\n153\t  focused button is removed from the DOM. This was a spec decision, not an\n154\t  oversight \u2014 flagging it here in case the reviewer's manual/Playwright\n155\t  pass wants to double check it still feels acceptable in practice.\n156\t- The re-sort-after-priority-change edge case needed no code change, as the\n157\t  spec predicted: `key={task.id}` in `TaskList.jsx` already lets React\n158\t  reconcile a reordered row into the same DOM node, so a focused control's\n159\t  focus survives a priority-driven reorder automatically.\n160\t- No ARIA attributes, disabled-control behaviour, or non-focus-indicator\n161\t  visual styling were touched, per the spec's non-goals.\n162\t\n163\t## How to verify\n164\t\n165\t```bash\n166\tcd frontend && npm run build   # type/build check \u2014 passes\n167\tcd frontend && npm run lint    # oxlint \u2014 could not be run in this session,\n168\t                                # see \"Known limitations\" below\n169\t```\n170\t\n171\tThere is still no frontend test runner (`frontend/package.json` has no\n172\t`test` script, no test files exist) \u2014 this predates this cycle per the\n173\tspec's note to the reviewer, and no test infra setup was in scope here.\n174\tJS logic (the focus-fallback computation, the registry, the edit-focus\n175\teffect's mount guard) was verified by tracing through each acceptance\n176\tcriterion's scenario by hand against the code rather than by running\n177\tautomated tests, since none exist to run yet.\n178\t\n179\tManual/keyboard verification path for the reviewer:\n180\t1. Load the app \u2014 focus should be in the task-title input (AC1).\n181\t2. Tab through every control listed in AC2, including a filter button\n182\t   toggled active and a task set to High priority, and confirm each shows a\n183\t   visible ring (Tab to `.filter-option.active` and to a High-priority\n184\t   `<select>` specifically, since those are the two with the CSS deviation\n185\t   above).\n186\t3. Add a few tasks, Tab to a middle row's Delete button, press Enter/Space \u2014\n187\t   focus should land on the next row's Delete button (AC4).\n188\t4. Repeat, deleting the last remaining row \u2014 focus should land back on the\n189\t   task-title input.\n190\t5. With the filter on \"Open\", complete a middle row via its Done button \u2014\n191\t   focus should land on the next row's Done button, or its Delete button if\n192\t   that row is already done (AC5).\n193\t6. With the filter on \"All\", complete a task \u2014 the row stays, only Done\n194\t   disappears; no keyboard trap is claimed here per spec.\n195\t7. Open a row's editor, press Escape \u2014 focus should land back on that row's\n196\t   Edit button (AC6). Repeat via Cancel, and again via Save (AC7).\n197\t\n198\t## Known limitations\n199\t\n200\t- Lint (`npm run lint`, which runs `oxlint`) could not be executed in this\n201\t  session \u2014 every invocation attempt (via `npm run lint`, the `oxlint`\n202\t  binary directly, and via its Node wrapper) was blocked by this\n203\t  environment's command-approval sandboxing before it could run, regardless\n204\t  of invocation form. `npm run build` (Vite's production build, which\n205\t  type-checks JSX via esbuild) ran successfully and is clean. The reviewer\n206\t  should run `npm run lint` directly if their environment allows it.\n207\t- The `:focus-visible` deviation above (inset outline for\n208\t  `.filter-option.active` / `.priority-select.priority-high`) is reasoned\n209\t  through geometrically, not visually confirmed \u2014 no browser/screenshot\n210\t  tooling was available in this session.\n211\t", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01NfMuSYJyojEL1gY8MFdPa1", "session_id": "7bff2ea0-c8d4-4a7a-97be-f850e3da5447"}}
{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:52:55Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 15, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\t# Spec: Make the app usable from the keyboard\n2\t\n3\t**Taiga:** #14\n4\t**Type:** Feature\n5\t**Branch:** feature/tp-14/make-app-usable-from-keyboard\n6\t\n7\t*Note: this overwrites `docs/spec.md` and `docs/design.md` from the previous\n8\tcycle (Taiga #12, give tasks a due date), which is already merged (`3c8a6a5`\n9\t/ `2b6cbd5`, working tree clean at dispatch time). That's expected \u2014 `docs/`\n10\tis scratch state scoped to the current cycle, not permanent documentation.*\n11\t\n12\t## Goal\n13\t\n14\tLet me work through my whole task list \u2014 add, search, filter, edit, set\n15\tpriority and due date, complete, delete \u2014 without ever touching the mouse.\n16\t\n17\t## Current state (why this is smaller than it sounds)\n18\t\n19\tEvery interactive control in the app is already a native, semantically\n20\tcorrect element (`<button>`, `<input>`, `<select>`, `<form>`) \u2014 there is no\n21\t`<div onClick>` or click-only affordance anywhere in\n22\t`frontend/src/components/`. That means basic reachability and operability by\n23\tTab/Enter/Space/arrow-keys is already true for essentially every control.\n24\tNothing in `frontend/src/App.css` resets `outline`, so the browser's default\n25\tfocus ring is intact everywhere too.\n26\t\n27\tWhat's actually missing, against the four things asked for:\n28\t\n29\t1. **\"Focus should land somewhere sensible on load\"** \u2014 nothing sets initial\n30\t   focus today; on load, focus sits on `<body>`, so the first Tab press\n31\t   starts from the very top of the page every time.\n32\t2. **\"Every control reachable and operable by keyboard\"** \u2014 true today,\n33\t   *except* focus is silently dropped to `<body>` after three specific\n34\t   keyboard-driven actions (see Acceptance Criteria 4-5), which forces a\n35\t   keyboard-only user to Tab back down from the top to find their place\n36\t   again. That's not a reachability gap in the strict sense (everything is\n37\t   still in the tab order), but it breaks the \"work through my list without\n38\t   touching the mouse\" goal in practice, so it's in scope.\n39\t3. **\"Focused element clearly visible\"** \u2014 true via the browser default ring\n40\t   on plain-background controls, but two control states set a background\n41\t   colour close to (or matching) typical default focus-ring colours \u2014\n42\t   `.filter-option.active` (`frontend/src/App.css:75`, near-black\n43\t   background) and `.priority-select.priority-high`\n44\t   (`frontend/src/App.css:107-111`, dark orange background) \u2014 where a thin\n45\t   default ring is genuinely hard to see. Needs an explicit, guaranteed-\n46\t   visible `:focus-visible` treatment.\n47\t4. **\"Nothing reachable only by clicking\"** \u2014 already true (see above); this\n48\t   spec's job here is to keep it true, not to fix a violation.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance criteria\n51\t\n52\t1. **Given** the app has finished its initial load, **when** the page\n53\t   first renders, **then** keyboard focus is on the task-title input in\n54\t   `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx:26-31` \u2014 the natural starting point\n55\t   for adding to or working through the list without a mouse. (Open\n56\t   Question 1.)\n57\t2. **Given** any interactive control in the app (add-task title/priority/\n58\t   due-date/submit, search input, filter buttons, clear-completed button,\n59\t   and per-row: priority select, due-date input, clear-due-date button,\n60\t   edit/done/delete buttons, and the edit-mode title input/save/cancel),\n61\t   **when** a user tabs to it and it has focus, **then** it shows a clearly\n62\t   visible focus indicator with sufficient contrast against its own\n63\t   background \u2014 including `.filter-option.active` and\n64\t   `.priority-select.priority-high`/`.priority-select.priority-low`.\n65\t3. **Given** any interactive control, **when** it has focus, **then** it can\n66\t   be activated using only the keyboard (Enter/Space for buttons, typing for\n67\t   text/date inputs, arrow keys + Enter/Space for selects) with the same\n68\t   effect as a mouse click \u2014 this is a regression check, not new behaviour,\n69\t   since it's already true.\n70\t4. **Given** a task row's Delete button has keyboard focus, **when** it is\n71\t   activated with Enter or Space, **then** after the row is removed, focus\n72\t   moves to the next task row's Delete button if one exists, otherwise to\n73\t   the previous row's Delete button if one exists, otherwise (list now\n74\t   empty) to the task-title input in `AddTask` \u2014 never left on `<body>`.\n75\t5. **Given** a task row's Done button has keyboard focus and activating it\n76\t   removes the row from view under the current filter (filter is \"Open\",\n77\t   `frontend/src/components/TaskFilter.jsx`), **when** it is activated,\n78\t   **then** focus moves the same way as Acceptance Criterion 4 (next row's\n79\t   Done-or-Delete-position control, else previous, else the AddTask title\n80\t   input). If the current filter keeps the row visible (\"All\" or \"Done\"),\n81\t   focus simply stays on that row's (now-absent) Done button's position \u2014\n82\t   no special handling needed since the button itself just disappears and\n83\t   the row stays, matching Delete's \"row gone\" case only when the row\n84\t   itself is gone.\n85\t6. **Given** a task is being edited (`frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx`\n86\t   `isEditing` state) and has focus inside the edit form, **when** the user\n87\t   presses Escape or clicks/activates Cancel, **then** focus returns to that\n88\t   same task's Edit button once the form closes, so the user doesn't lose\n89\t   their place in the list.\n90\t7. **Given** a task is being edited, **when** the user submits a valid title\n91\t   (Save, or Enter per the existing handler at\n92\t   `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx:77-83`), **then** focus returns to\n93\t   that same task's Edit button once the form closes \u2014 same reasoning as\n94\t   Acceptance Criterion 6.\n95\t\n96\t## Non-goals\n97\t\n98\t- No new ARIA labelling beyond what already exists \u2014 `aria-label`,\n99\t  `aria-live`, `role=\"status\"`, `role=\"alert\"`, and `role=\"group\"` are\n100\t  already present throughout (e.g. `TaskList.jsx:89,108,117,124`,\n101\t  `TaskFilter.jsx:9`, `App.jsx:65`) and are not part of this request.\n102\t- No custom global keyboard shortcuts (e.g. a hotkey to jump to \"add task\"\n103\t  or to a specific filter). The request asks for standard Tab/Enter/Space\n104\t  operability, not new shortcuts.\n105\t- No \"skip to content\" link. The app is a single short view (heading, one\n106\t  form, one toolbar, one list) with no repeated navigation to skip past.\n107\t- No change to visual design beyond the focus indicator itself \u2014 colours,\n108\t  spacing, and layout are otherwise untouched.\n109\t- No change to disabled-control behaviour (e.g. the disabled Add/Save/Clear-\n110\t  completed buttons). Disabled controls are equally unreachable by mouse and\n111\t  keyboard today, so they're not a \"mouse-only\" gap.\n112\t- No backend changes \u2014 this is entirely a frontend concern.\n113\t\n114\t## Affected areas\n115\t\n116\t**Frontend only**\n117\t- `frontend/src/components/AddTask.jsx` \u2014 add `autoFocus` to the task-title\n118\t  input (AC1); accept a `ref`/imperative handle (or the App-level focus call\n119\t  targets this input directly) so focus can be returned to it from\n120\t  `App.jsx` when the list empties out (AC4).\n121\t- `frontend/src/components/TaskList.jsx` \u2014 the `TaskRow` component needs:\n122\t  after Delete/Done removes a row, move focus per AC4/AC5 (likely via a\n123\t  callback the row calls just before/after invoking `onDelete`/`onComplete`,\n124\t  since the row itself is about to unmount and can't move focus once it's\n125\t  gone); after Escape/Cancel/Save closes edit mode, return focus to the\n126\t  Edit button (AC6/AC7) \u2014 the Edit button already exists at\n127\t  `TaskList.jsx:134`, needs a stable ref to focus.\n128\t- `frontend/src/App.jsx` \u2014 owns the task array and knows sibling order, so\n129\t  it's the natural place to compute \"which row/control gets focus next\" for\n130\t  AC4/AC5 (TaskRow alone doesn't know about neighbouring rows) and to expose\n131\t  a way to focus the AddTask input for the \"list is now empty\" and initial-\n132\t  load cases.\n133\t- `frontend/src/App.css` \u2014 add an explicit `:focus-visible` rule (or rules)\n134\t  giving every interactive element a guaranteed-visible outline, including\n135\t  extra contrast handling for `.filter-option.active` and\n136\t  `.priority-select.priority-high`/`.priority-select.priority-low`.\n137\t\n138\t## Edge cases\n139\t\n140\t- **Empty list on load:** AC1 still applies \u2014 focus goes to the AddTask\n141\t  title input regardless of whether there are zero, one, or many tasks.\n142\t- **Deleting the only remaining task:** falls through AC4's last case \u2014\n143\t  focus goes to the AddTask title input, not `<body>`.\n144\t- **Deleting the first or last row in a multi-row list:** AC4 covers both\n145\t  directions (next, falling back to previous).\n146\t- **Completing a task while filter is \"All\" or \"Done\":** row stays visible,\n147\t  only the Done button disappears from that row \u2014 no focus-loss scenario\n148\t  since the row (and its Delete button) is still there; see AC5's second\n149\t  case.\n150\t- **Completing a task while filter is \"Open\":** row disappears \u2014 same\n151\t  handling as delete (AC5's first case).\n152\t- **Search narrows the list to exclude the focused row's task:** out of\n153\t  scope for this spec. Typing in the search input keeps focus in the search\n154\t  input itself (the row disappearing doesn't strand focus, since focus\n155\t  never left the search box) \u2014 no new handling needed.\n156\t- **Escape during edit with an empty draft (validation error showing):**\n157\t  Escape still cancels and discards the draft per the existing `cancel()`\n158\t  behaviour (`TaskList.jsx:54-58`); AC6 still applies \u2014 focus returns to\n159\t  Edit.\n160\t- **Re-sorted list after a priority change moves a row's position:** React\n161\t  reconciles by `task.id` (`TaskList.jsx:30`, stable `key`), so the DOM node\n162\t  and its focus survive the reorder automatically \u2014 already correct, no\n163\t  change needed, called out here so the reviewer knows it's a verified\n164\t  no-op rather than an overlooked case.\n165\t- **Focus-visible contrast on every button colour variant:** plain white\n166\t  buttons, the dark `.filter-option.active`, and both `.priority-select`\n167\t  colour variants all need to be checked individually (AC2), not just the\n168\t  default case.\n169\t\n170\t## Open questions\n171\t\n172\t1. **Where exactly should initial focus land \u2014 the AddTask title input, or\n173\t   somewhere else (e.g. the search box, or the first task row)?** Proceeding\n174\t   under: the AddTask title input (AC1). Reasoning: the stated goal is\n175\t   working through the list \"without touching the mouse,\" and creating a\n176\t   task is the one action available even when the list is empty, making it\n177\t   the only universally-sensible default. If the intent was instead \"land\n178\t   on the list itself so I can immediately act on existing tasks,\" that's a\n179\t   one-line change to what receives `autoFocus`.\n180\t2. **Should Tab order be changed from DOM order (e.g. to visually reflect\n181\t   priority sort order), or is DOM order acceptable?** Proceeding under: DOM\n182\t   order is acceptable and unchanged \u2014 the existing markup order already\n183\t   matches the visual layout top-to-bottom, left-to-right, which is the\n184\t   expected/unsurprising tab order; nothing in the request asks for a\n185\t   different traversal order, only that everything be reachable.\n186\t\n187\t## Note for